> **Kotlin 2.4+:** collapse the `_uiState`/`uiState` pair with explicit backing fields (`val uiState: StateFlow<FooUiState>` + `field = MutableStateFlow(…)`) — `uiState` only, never the effects `Channel`. Full idiom + version gate: `android-skills:kotlin-flows`.

### Testing

Drive the ViewModel through its `FooActions` methods — never through a sealed-`Intent` dispatcher — and assert on `uiState`/`effects` with Turbine:

```kotlin
@Test
fun `onRefresh loads items into state`() = runTest {
    coEvery { getItemsUseCase() } returns Result.success(items)
    val viewModel = FooViewModel(getItemsUseCase)

    viewModel.uiState.test {
        awaitItem()                       // initial state

        viewModel.onRefresh()              // discrete method call, not onIntent(Intent.Refresh)

        awaitItem().isLoading shouldBe true
        awaitItem().items shouldBe items
    }
}

@Test
fun `onItemClick emits a NavigateTo effect`() = runTest {
    val viewModel = FooViewModel(getItemsUseCase)

    viewModel.effects.test {
        viewModel.onItemClick("42")        // discrete method call

        awaitItem() shouldBe FooEffect.NavigateTo("42")
    }
}
```

Because the ViewModel implements `FooActions` directly, tests call `viewModel.onItemClick(id)` / `viewModel.onRefresh()` like the UI does — there's no intermediate `Intent` type to construct or exhaustively branch on. Full test setup (Turbine dependency, `MainDispatcherRule`, fakes vs. mocks): `android-skills:android-testing`.

